cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Value-Field Theory (VFT)"
message: "If you use this theory or repository, please cite this repository."
authors:
  - family-names: "Nuno"
    given-names: "T."
    affiliation: "Independent Researcher"
abstract: >
  Value-Field Theory (VFT) models economic and organizational activity through
  a physical or real-resource state K, actor-specific exchange-value and capital
  representations K_i, structured subjective states P_i, and actor-side actions
  or processes A_i. The field is not an independent scalar value; it is the
  actor-resource configuration of resources, subjective states, capital
  representations, relations, and constraints that conditions, induces, and
  reproduces action. VFT distinguishes realized use-value from exchange-value:
  use-value quantity refers to utility realized through use over an interval,
  while exchange-value is a comparative representation that may describe both
  point-in-time positions and interval transactions. These two value semantics
  are therefore not identified one-to-one with stock and flow. Delta K denotes
  endpoint change in the physical resource state, while gross interval activity
  remains in the action or event history. K_i is not a subset or partition of K
  and is not identified with a balance sheet; formal ledgers, profit-and-loss
  accounts, and balance sheets are accounting projections that may implement
  actor-specific monetary representations under explicit recognition, valuation,
  and accounting rules. Surplus is not a physical primitive but a difference or
  residual that becomes defined after physical or use-value activity is mapped
  into a comparable exchange-value scale. Subjective forecasts of resource-state
  change are derived from the belief or expectation component of P_i and are
  kept distinct from preference and plan. VFT defines a business entity as a
  local action-generating field structure that continuously induces and
  reproduces activity, allowing firms, NPOs, public businesses, and state or
  municipal activities to be described without making profit maximization the
  universal definition of business. Entrepreneurship and new-business
  development can therefore be represented as field formation, while existing
  business operation is field maintenance, reproduction, and improvement. VFT
  retains resource-realization, activity-flow, and P-downside as constitutive
  rationality assumptions and allows Marxian categories such as use-value,
  labor, exchange-value, surplus, and accumulation to be represented through
  projection-specific specializations.
